# .uis.extend/neko-exposure.yaml
#
# HOW THIS INSTALLATION EXPOSES NEKO'S WEBRTC MEDIA.
#
# Per-installation config, not product config — the same way
# prometheus-targets.yaml holds this installation's scrape targets. UIS ships
# neko; where its video can be reached from depends on the network it is in.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
#
# neko's web view is plain HTTP + websocket and reaches users through Traefik
# like everything else. Its *media* cannot: WebRTC needs raw TCP, and neko
# advertises a single address:port pair to the browser through ICE
# (NEKO_WEBRTC_NAT1TO1 : NEKO_WEBRTC_TCPMUX). The browser then connects to
# exactly that. So the advertised address must be one the VIEWER can reach —
# which is a property of the installation, not of the product.
#
# Get it wrong and the failure is quiet: the page loads, the pod is Ready,
# /health is 200, and the screen stays black forever.
#
# THE DEFAULT NEEDS NO EDITING on Rancher Desktop.
#
# mode: local
#   A NodePort on 32816, advertised as 127.0.0.1:32816. Rancher Desktop maps a
#   NodePort straight onto localhost, so this works with zero configuration.
#   The port number is deliberately identical on both sides — see below.
#
# mode: tailscale
#   For a cluster with the Tailscale operator (the reference installation).
#   The Service becomes a tailscale LoadBalancer and the media is advertised at
#   the tailnet address. Use this when viewers are on the tailnet rather than on
#   the machine running the cluster.
#
#   ⚠️ advertise_address must be the tailnet IP the Service ends up owning. It
#   cannot be discovered before the LoadBalancer exists, so it is stated here.
#   Set it, deploy, and confirm with `./uis verify neko`.

mode: local

# --- only read when mode: tailscale ---
# advertise_address: "100.97.5.40"
# hostname: "neko"

# --- advanced, both modes ---
# The port neko listens on for muxed WebRTC TCP, AND the port it advertises.
# ⚠️ These are the same number by necessity. A NodePort that renumbered the
# port would advertise a port the viewer cannot reach, and the symptom would be
# a black screen with everything reporting healthy. If you change this, keep it
# inside the cluster's NodePort range (30000-32767 by default).
# tcpmux_port: 32816
